# AMD64 thunk export spec -- msvcr100.dll.
#
# From the 2026-08-17 corpus pass.  Styx: Master of Shadows is built against
# the VC++ 2010 runtime and imports 289 exports of this module STATICALLY --
# as do eight of the wxWidgets DLLs it ships, and nvtt_64, libogg_64,
# libvorbis_64 and libvorbisfile_64 -- and it had no guest thunk, so the
# loader found the native ppc64 module, correctly refused to splice it into a
# guest call (c000007b), and the whole image failed to load (c0000135) before
# one guest instruction ran.
#
# 796 of 1187 eligible exports are emitted from this module's own .spec,
# re-measured 2026-08-29 (the "766 of 1185"/"419 refusals" this banner
# originally claimed had drifted from reality well before this file's
# ExceptionObject trio correction below -- this module's surface has grown a
# few PROBE-EXTRA/GUEST-IMPL rows since the 2026-08-17 corpus pass and nobody
# had re-measured the totals; not itself part of that correction, just
# caught alongside it).  The 391 refusals are named classes, not guesses --
# the printf/scanf family (true varargs, which need the variadic= column and
# a per-name v-variant); the MSVC C++ EH ABI (_CxxThrowException,
# __CxxFrameHandler{,2,3}, __RTDynamicCast), which is the guest image's OWN
# language personality and must not be sent into the host unwinder, exactly
# as vcruntime140.__CxxFrameHandler3 is a documented hole in
# ppc64le/thunks/check-import-chain.sh; the C++ mangled names, which are not
# flat prototypes at all; and div/ldiv/lldiv/_cabs, which pass a struct by
# value.  Each keeps its slot as a named sentinel.  __Cxx{Register,
# Unregister}ExceptionObject and __DestructExceptionObject are NOT part of
# this refused set -- see the EXCLUDE lines and their correction below for
# why they need their own, more recently added, mechanism to get the same
# named-sentinel outcome this banner has always claimed for them.
DLL msvcr100.dll
FROM-SPEC auto

# Seven headers the oracle's default translation unit does not reach, and this
# module's surface is declared across all of them.  Measured (before the
# msvcrt.h addition below): they take the emitted count from 666 to 766 -- a
# hundred exports that were named sentinels purely because nothing had told
# the oracle where to look.  Each is here because a name in THIS .spec needed
# it, not on speculation:
#
#   locale.h      setlocale, localeconv, _configthreadlocale
#   setjmp.h      longjmp
#   sys/utime.h   _wutime64 and the _utime family
#   sys/timeb.h   _ftime64
#   mbstring.h    _ismbblead and the _mbs* surface
#   mtdll.h       _lock / _unlock -- the CRT's own locks, declared in no
#                 public header at all.  msvcrt.thunks reaches them the same
#                 way; INCLUDE-DIR points at ../msvcrt because that is this
#                 module's PARENTSRC and where its implementation lives.
#   msvcrt.h      __dllonexit -- same story as _lock/_unlock: declared only in
#                 msvcrt's own private header (msvcrt.thunks and
#                 ucrtbase.thunks both PROBE-EXTRA it for the same name).
#                 Without it __dllonexit had no declaration this module's
#                 oracle could see and was emitted as a named sentinel -- a
#                 guest calling it landed on 0xDEAD00nn instead of the real
#                 export.  Styx: Master of Shadows imports it (its bundled
#                 wxmsw28u_vc_custom_64.dll calls it to grow its own private
#                 onexit table) and died there once the exports before it on
#                 its import list were fixed.  __dllonexit itself never CALLS
#                 the function pointer it is handed -- it only reallocs the
#                 caller's table and stores the pointer -- so correct arity is
#                 the whole fix; no ntdll override row is needed for this one,
#                 unlike _onexit below.
INCLUDE-DIR   ../msvcrt
PROBE-EXTRA   locale.h
PROBE-EXTRA   setjmp.h
PROBE-EXTRA   sys/utime.h
PROBE-EXTRA   sys/timeb.h
PROBE-EXTRA   mtdll.h
PROBE-EXTRA   msvcrt.h
PROBE-EXTRA   mbstring.h

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-29, guest-cxx-eh-plan Session A follow-up (adversarial review):
# the same __C_specific_handler identity bug and ExceptionObject landmine
# vcruntime140/ucrtbase carried, also live here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORRECTION to the banner at the top of this file: it has claimed since the
# 2026-08-17 corpus pass that __Cxx{Register,Unregister}ExceptionObject
# "keeps its slot as a named sentinel" alongside __CxxFrameHandler3 and
# _CxxThrowException.  That was FALSE for those two (and for
# __DestructExceptionObject, not even named in that list) from the day this
# file was written: msvcr100.spec:728-730 declares all three, msvcrt.h:156-
# 158 declares all three, PROBE-EXTRA msvcrt.h above has always carried that
# declaration into this oracle's view, and FROM-SPEC auto has no way to know
# a declared name is meant to stay refused -- it emitted ordinary native trap
# stubs, MEASURED at RVA 0xe0e0/0xe0f0/0xe100 in the built msvcr100.dll.  The
# EXCLUDE lines below are what actually makes the banner's claim true.
#
# msvcr100.spec:715 similarly declares __C_specific_handler (MEASURED trap at
# RVA 0xe0d0) -- the exact identity-defeating shape the 2026-08-29 triage
# fixed for vcruntime140/ucrtbase: dlls/ntdll/signal_ppc64.c's frame walk
# recognises __C_specific_handler by ADDRESS IDENTITY against the guest
# ntdll's own export, so a VS2010-era /MD title (this module's whole reason
# for existing) whose .xdata handler resolves through THIS module's trap
# would be entered as unknown guest code and trap into native ppc64 with an
# AMD64 DISPATCHER_CONTEXT it cannot interpret.  Same fix: forward to ntdll's
# own copy.
FORWARD __C_specific_handler ntdll.__C_specific_handler

EXCLUDE __CxxRegisterExceptionObject
EXCLUDE __CxxUnregisterExceptionObject
EXCLUDE __DestructExceptionObject

# _initterm walks a table of the CALLER's initializers.  For a guest caller
# those entries are x86-64 code and this walker is ppc64, so the guest import
# is redirected to a form that wraps each entry as it is reached.  A callback
# mask cannot express this: the argument is a pointer to an ARRAY of function
# pointers, not a function pointer.  Identical to the pair
# dlls/msvcrt/msvcrt.thunks and dlls/ucrtbase/ucrtbase.thunks already carry,
# and it needs the same two `-arch=ppc64` forwards in msvcr100.spec beside
# _initterm_e, which this change adds.
#
# MEASURED, which is why these two lines exist rather than being anticipated.
# Styx: Master of Shadows, 2026-08-17, once the fifteen missing thunk modules
# let it past loader_init for the first time: its own bundled
# wxmsw28u_vc_custom_64.dll reached PROCESS_ATTACH, its DllMain called
# MSVCR100._initterm_e with a table at 0x1001CCBC0..0x1001CCBD0, and NATIVE
# msvcr100 bctrl'd into the first entry --
#
#   trace:seh:find_guest_thunk_target L"MSVCR100.dll"._initterm_e -> (2 args)
#   trace:seh:dispatch_exception code=c0000005 addr=00000001001BE2E4
#     iar=00000001001be2e4  ctr=00000001001be2e4  lr=00003fffff1cc900
#
# ctr and iar are the same GUEST address (inside wxmsw28u at base
# 0x100110000) while lr is inside native msvcr100's _initterm_e: the walker
# jumped into x86-64 bytes.  loader_init then aborted the process, c0000005.
# Exactly the failure README records for msvcrt and ucrtbase, in the one C
# runtime that had never been given a guest thunk.  With these two lines the
# same run reaches __wine_guest__initterm_e, runs the guest initializers as
# guest code, and stops on the next thing instead.
GUEST-IMPL   _initterm                        __wine_guest__initterm
GUEST-IMPL   _initterm_e                      __wine_guest__initterm_e

# ONE .spec-location override, and it is earned by a measured call rather than
# anticipated -- which is the rule this pass held to everywhere (see
# dlls/xapofx1_5/xapofx1_5.thunks for a hole deliberately left because nothing
# had called it yet).
#
# With _initterm_e fixed, the very next thing the same Styx run did was call
# the sentinel:
#
#   err:seh:dispatch_guest_exception guest called through a wild pointer:
#     00000000DEAD0008 is in no guest image; the call was made from
#     00000001001BE2F5 = L"wxmsw28u_vc_custom_64.dll"+ae2f5
#   warn:module:import_dll No implementation for MSVCR100.dll._malloc_crt ...
#     setting to 00000000DEAD0008
#
# _malloc_crt is the CRT's internal allocator and no Wine header declares it,
# but nothing here is a guess about its shape: msvcr100.spec:1243 is
# `@ cdecl _malloc_crt(long) malloc`, so it IS malloc -- one size_t argument,
# a pointer return -- and dlls/msvcrt/heap.c:430 `void* CDECL malloc(size_t)`
# is the implementation the forward names.  The .spec location in the last
# column is the documented downgrade (see dlls/msvcp140/msvcp140.thunks): the
# <int|void> column stops being machine-checked and becomes a human assertion,
# and the assertion is that a pointer return is integer class.
_malloc_crt                   0x0000A110  1  int   msvcr100.spec:1243

# host->guest callback -- CLOSED for this module.  Read the banner in
# dlls/msvcrt/msvcrt.thunks before debugging a crash here.  The
# registration-interception table in dlls/ntdll/signal_ppc64.c lists
# msvcr100.dll beside msvcrt.dll and ucrtbase.dll for qsort's comparator,
# bsearch's, and _onexit's registered handler -- the three rows msvcrt and
# ucrtbase already carried and this module was missing.  __dllonexit is
# covered too, but by construction rather than by a row: it never calls the
# guest pointer it is handed (see the PROBE-EXTRA msvcrt.h note above), so
# fixing its arity was the whole fix.
#
# The five this file used to list as STILL OPEN now have rows as well, so
# there is no longer a raw guest pointer leaving this module for native code
# to bctrl.  Styx: Master of Shadows imports all five.  What each row had to
# get right, since none of it follows from the registering export's own
# arity:
#
#   _beginthread          start routine at argument 0, `void (*)(void *)`.
#   _beginthreadex        start routine at argument 2, `unsigned (__stdcall *)
#                         (void *)`.  __stdcall is a no-op on x86-64, so the
#                         real difference from _beginthread is the return, and
#                         it still takes the default width because
#                         _endthreadex reads 32 bits of it.  Neither of these
#                         two is reached by the invocation-time thread-start
#                         interception: msvcrt's own native trampoline is what
#                         CreateThread is given, and it calls the guest
#                         routine itself.
#   _set_invalid_parameter_handler
#                         the only FIVE-argument callback in the table.  At
#                         the default arity the trampoline's identity register
#                         is r7, which is exactly where native
#                         _invalid_parameter has already put pReserved -- so a
#                         four-argument slot would not fault, it would hand the
#                         handler a wrong value.  That is what the cb_argc
#                         column added for this row exists to prevent.
#   _set_purecall_handler `void (*)(void)`, no arguments at all; the default
#                         slot is right because there is nothing to overwrite.
#   __setusermatherr      `int (*)(struct _exception *)`, and the one whose
#                         RETURN is read -- dlls/msvcrt/math.c tests it as
#                         "I handled it" -- so the default sign-extended
#                         32-bit return is required, not merely harmless.
#
# The proof is ppc64le/seh/check-crt-callbacks.sh, which registers all five
# from a guest, makes native CRT code call each one, and checks the VALUES
# each callback received and returned against a native control; it goes red
# under WINEEMUNOCBWRAP=1.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FOUR C++ MEMORY OPERATORS, and why these four and not the rest of the
# mangled surface.
#
# The blanket refusal above -- "the C++ mangled names, which are not flat
# prototypes at all" -- is right about the CLASS and wrong about these four.
# `void *operator new(size_t)` and `void operator delete(void *)` are flat
# prototypes with a mangled NAME: one integer-class argument, a pointer or
# nothing back, no `this`, no EH state, no language runtime.  What refused them
# is the signature oracle, which resolves a NAME against the translation unit
# it compiles and finds no header declaring `??2@YAPEAX_K@Z` -- a tooling gap,
# exactly the class ppc64le/games/STATUS.md's psapi/wldap32 handoff names,
# and not an ABI fact.  msvcr100.spec declares all four with their arity and
# their implementation, so the .spec location column is the documented
# downgrade for precisely this: the <int|void> column becomes a human
# assertion, checked against the .spec line's own argument list.
#
# [MEASURED] This is what Styx: Master of Shadows stops on.  With Proton's
# x86-64 msvcp100.dll staged into C:\windows\sysx8664 (ppc64le/steamtool/proton)
# every one of its 65 std:: imports binds for real and the
# "No implementation for MSVCP100.dll..." warnings go to ZERO -- and the game
# still dies in the same place, because the sentinel it calls was never
# msvcp100's:
#
#   No implementation for MSVCR100.dll.??2@YAPEAX_K@Z imported from
#     wxmsw28u_vc_custom_64.dll, setting to 00000000DEAD000B
#   guest called through a wild pointer: 00000000DEAD000B ... from
#     L"wxmsw28u_vc_custom_64.dll"+5d580
#
# operator new, called from wxWidgets' PROCESS_ATTACH.  ppc64le/games/STATUS.md
# read that same 0xDEAD000B as msvcp100's; the sentinel numbering is per
# IMPORTING module, so the index says nothing about which DLL owns the name.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE.  __CxxFrameHandler3 and _CxxThrowException
# stay refused: they are the guest image's own language personality and must
# not be sent into the host unwinder, exactly as the banner above says and
# exactly as vcruntime140.__CxxFrameHandler3 is a documented hole in
# ppc64le/thunks/check-import-chain.sh.  _HUGE and _encoded_null are `@ extern`
# DATA exports, and a trap stub cannot serve data -- there is no call to trap
# on -- which is the design item ppc64le/games/STATUS.md already records.
??2@YAPEAX_K@Z    0x00000C01  1  int   msvcr100.spec:297
??_U@YAPEAX_K@Z   0x00000C02  1  int   msvcr100.spec:347
??3@YAXPEAX@Z     0x00000C03  1  void  msvcr100.spec:301
??_V@YAXPEAX@Z    0x00000C04  1  void  msvcr100.spec:351

# THE OTHER TWO NAMES msvcp100's DllMain LOOKS UP, and they belong with the
# four above because they arrive by the same route: a staged x86-64 msvcp100
# (ppc64le/steamtool/proton provisions one) runs GetProcAddress over msvcr100
# at PROCESS_ATTACH for four mangled names -- operator new, operator delete,
# and these two.  The first two are served by the rows above (their array
# siblings came along because they are the same shape).  Leaving these two
# refused would have been the quiet kind of wrong: GetProcAddress answers
# NULL, msvcp100 carries on, and what the guest loses is not a crash but a
# capability.
#
#   ?IsCurrentTaskCollectionCanceling@Context@Concurrency@@SA_NXZ
#       No arguments, returns `bool`.  A static member with no `this`, so it
#       is a flat prototype with a mangled name exactly as operator new is,
#       and dlls/msvcrt/concurrency.c:877 is the implementation
#       msvcr100.spec:386 forwards to.  `bool` is integer class; the int
#       column is the human assertion the .spec downgrade asks for.
#
#   ?_set_new_handler@@YAP6AH_K@ZP6AH0@Z@Z
#       One argument, and it is a GUEST FUNCTION POINTER -- `int (*)(size_t)`,
#       which native operator new calls when an allocation fails -- so the row
#       here is only half of it.  The other half is the registration-
#       interception entry in dlls/ntdll/signal_ppc64.c: without one, the raw
#       guest address goes to native msvcrt, whose LOCK_HEAP'd global keeps it
#       until the day an allocation fails and dlls/msvcrt/heap.c bctrl's into
#       x86-64 bytes -- at the worst possible moment, under memory pressure,
#       nowhere near the registration that caused it.
#
#       NOTE the -arch=win64 line.  msvcr100.spec declares this name four
#       times: a win32 and a win64 STUB (the `H` forms, taking an int) and a
#       win32 and a win64 real forward (the `P6AH...@Z` forms, taking the
#       handler).  Citing a line inside an -arch= group used to be refused
#       outright for exactly that reason; it is accepted now only because
#       spec2thunk re-runs winebuild's own -arch= filter over the whole .spec
#       and checks that line 598 IS the line this machine's export table keeps
#       for this name.  Cite the win32 line instead and it still refuses.
#
# Everything else on msvcr100's mangled surface stays refused, for the reason
# the banner above already gives: those are real member functions with a
# `this`, which is the MSVC C++ ABI this port does not translate.  What puts
# these two on the served side is not that they are wanted -- it is that they
# have no `this`, the same test the four operators passed.
?IsCurrentTaskCollectionCanceling@Context@Concurrency@@SA_NXZ  0x00000C05  0  int   msvcr100.spec:386
?_set_new_handler@@YAP6AH_K@ZP6AH0@Z@Z                         0x00000C06  1  int   msvcr100.spec:598
